course: dictionary
lesson: 14368
title: Oppressive: heavy, cruel, or weighing you down
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Oppressive is a word with three meanings, and the sentence tells you which one to use. It can describe a cruel ruler who keeps people down, hot and airless weather, or a feeling of being weighed down with worry or sadness.
explain: «The oppressive king took so much money that families had none left.» The clues are king, took and none left. Those tell you oppressive means cruel and unfair. Always ask: what does this word make me picture or feel?
explain: «The air was oppressive before the thunderstorm, hot and thick.» Here the clues are air, hot and thick, so oppressive means heavy and uncomfortable, like weather. Swap in your meaning and check that the sentence still makes sense.
explain: «An oppressive silence fell over the Wild Wood.» Nothing is cruel here. The silence weighs on you and makes you feel worried. When a word has more than one meaning, the words around it are your best guide.
fact: Oppressive is an adjective, so it describes a noun such as a ruler, a day or a silence.
fact: Oppressive has three meanings: cruel and unfair, hot and airless, or weighing you down with worry or sadness.
fact: «Oppressive» means cruel and unfair, keeping people down: «an oppressive ruler».
fact: «Oppressive» can describe weather that is hot, heavy and airless, as it often is before a storm.
fact: «Oppressive» can describe a feeling of being weighed down, as in «an oppressive silence».
exercise: «The people rose up against the oppressive ruler, who took away their freedom.» What does oppressive mean? | Bright and cheerful | *Cruel and unfair, keeping people down | Quick and clever | Kind and generous
exercise: «The afternoon was oppressive, so hot and heavy that the air felt thick.» What is being called oppressive? | A loud brass band | Soft rain on a window | *Hot, heavy air before a storm | A bright, cool breeze
exercise: «An oppressive silence fell over the Wild Wood.» What does the silence make you feel? | Bored of reading | *Worried and weighed down | Hungry for lunch | Excited and giggly
test: «The oppressive laws stopped people from voting.» Which meaning fits best here? | Bright and shiny | Short and quick | *Cruel and unfair, taking away freedom | Fair and kind
test: Which of these is oppressive weather? | A windy, rainy day | *A hot, heavy day with no wind | A cool, breezy day | A snowy, icy morning
exam: Ella says oppressive means happy, like opulent. What is the correct meaning? | Fast and loud | *Cruel, heavy or worrying, not happy | Very tiny | Happy and bright
